{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"broken-spirited-dorado","creation_channel":"mcp","description":"## Problem\nDiscovered during plan-review of the sibling \"rank aggregation by priority\" ticket\n(beaming-snively-urial): `src/rebar/llm/spec_scan.py:13,137` imports `_severity_rank` from\n`aggregate.py` directly and sorts its own findings list by it\n(`findings.sort(key=_severity_rank, reverse=True)`). `spec_scan.py`'s findings come straight\nfrom the \"spec-alignment\" LLM reviewer's structured output via `finalize_findings`\n(`findings.py`) -- they never pass through `pass3_decide` and never get `priority`, unlike\nthe review-kernel/DET-tier findings the rest of this epic migrates. `_severity_rank` reads\n`finding.get(\"severity\")`, so once the epic's final cleanup ticket\n(byzantine-spinelike-penguin) removes the `severity` field from `findings.py`'s\n`normalize_finding`, every spec_scan finding's severity read returns nothing,\n`_severity_rank` collapses to the same rank for all findings, and `rebar scan-spec` silently\nloses its result ordering. This is a distinct producer from the review-kernel/review-bot path\nthe rest of the epic covers, so it needs its own priority assignment rather than being folded\ninto the aggregation-ranking ticket.\n\n## Approach\nIn `spec_scan.py`, where a finding's severity is currently read for sorting, map its\n5-level `severity` string to a numeric `priority` using a fixed ordinal mapping consistent\nwith the vocabulary in `findings.py::SEVERITIES` (critical/high/medium/low/info): `critical\n-> 1.0, high -> 0.75, medium -> 0.5, low -> 0.25, info -> 0.0`. Assign this `priority` onto\neach finding dict (e.g. in `finalize_findings`'s per-finding processing, or immediately after,\ninside `spec_scan.py`) before the existing `findings.sort(...)` call, then change that call to\nsort by the new `priority` key instead of `_severity_rank`. This mirrors the sibling DET-tier\nticket's approach (fixed-mapping priority assignment for a producer that doesn't route through\n`pass3_decide`) rather than the LLM-kernel path.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Every finding `spec_scan.py`'s `scan_spec`/equivalent entry point returns carries a\n  `priority` float derived from its `severity` via the fixed mapping above.\n- [ ] `spec_scan.py`'s sort (currently `findings.sort(key=_severity_rank, reverse=True)`) is\n  replaced with a sort on the new `priority` field, producing the same relative order as\n  today for a mixed-severity findings list (critical before high before medium before low\n  before info).\n- [ ] `pytest tests/unit/test_spec_scan.py` (or the actual test module covering `spec_scan.py`,\n  located via `pytest --collect-only -q -k spec_scan`) passes, with a strengthened test\n  asserting the `priority` field's value for each severity level and the resulting sort order.\n- [ ] `grep -n \"_severity_rank\" src/rebar/llm/spec_scan.py` returns no matches (spec_scan.py no\n  longer depends on the shared `_severity_rank` helper).\n\n## Testing\nTier: in-process unit test against the real `spec_scan` finding-processing/sort logic (pure\ncalculation on in-memory finding dicts, no I/O). Oracle: the `priority` value assigned to a\nfinding of each severity level, and the sorted order of a mixed list, asserted exactly.\nContrast case: a mixed-severity list (one finding per level) sorted once before this change\n(by `_severity_rank`) and once after (by `priority`) must produce the identical relative\norder.","id":"0275-ded6-ded5-4fbe","parent_id":"c4c8-317a-85ca-4b08","priority":2,"repos":["rebar"],"tags":[],"ticket_type":"task","title":"Give spec_scan.py findings a priority before severity is removed"},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1788127007863683765,"uuid":"aad42326-86c0-41b3-8939-255d7be63b6d"}